armhf is a component build
==========================

Why
---

The final link of chrome does not fit in a 32-bit address space. lld needs
3.25 GB of address space for a target that carries about half of chrome's
input, against about 4 GiB for an AArch32 process. That was measured by
bisecting RLIMIT_AS on a replayed link. On the buildd it ends as:

  ld.lld-22: error: failed to open ./chrome: Cannot allocate memory

A component build links each library on its own, so no single link needs
that much address space.

armhf had no chromium between 150.0.7871.181-1, built on 2026-07-23, and
this change. The bug is https://bugs.debian.org/1150706 .

What was ruled out first
------------------------

ld.bfd, ld.gold and mold all needed more memory than lld, not less.

Linking on a 64-bit host does work, and it was proposed twice:

  https://salsa.debian.org/chromium-team/chromium/-/merge_requests/44
  https://salsa.debian.org/chromium-team/chromium/-/merge_requests/45

Both need a build dependency from another architecture, which wanna-build
cannot express. That request was declined on 2026-10-06:

  https://bugs.debian.org/cgi-bin/bugreport.cgi?bug=770925#44

What to check on a version bump
-------------------------------

- The browser starts, on a machine that can execute armhf.
- The largest link still fits. To check it without a buildd round trip,
  replay that link on an x86_64 host with a cross linker and bisect
  RLIMIT_AS.
- The packages are produced even when the lintian step after them fails.
  Under qemu, lintian fails by running out of memory.
